i love cool lesser talked about types of  training that can help you to achieve  awesome results isometrics ticks all  those boxes and then some and in this  post i'm going to share two even lesser  known forms of isometrics that might  make a huge difference to your strength  gains  but first a quick recap  so isometrics are contractions with no  movement these come in two flavors  overcoming isometrics and yielding  isometrics the former refers to an  isometric contraction against an  immovable object trying to bend an iron  bar for instance or push down a tree  like the indian wrestler gamma  the latter kind yielding isometrics  refers to any attempt to hold a position  against resistance such as when holding  a handstand or keeping a barbell halfway  above your chest with arms bent during a  bench press  both kinds of contraction have their  benefits but it's overcoming isometrics  that will help you to increase your max  strength without necessarily increasing  muscle size  this is accomplished by increasing your  ability to recruit motor units in the  muscle in other words the nervous system  adapts to enable you to use your muscles  more totally  this also occurs during one rep max here  you use 100 of your available force to  move a weight thus training yourself to  use your muscle at maximum efficiency  but the limitation here is that during a  one rep maximum that maximum force  within the muscle only occurs for a  couple of milliseconds  at the most difficult point of the  movement  what sets an overcoming isometric apart  is that this maximum contraction is  continuous allowing for a truly max  effort over an extended period  the results speak for themselves most  old-time strongmen used some form of  overcoming isometrics modern strongman  dennis rodgers has done the same thing  to the point of exceeding even elite  athletes in his motor recruitment  as i've discussed often this was also a  key component of bruce lee's training  to utilize overcoming isometrics to  their fullest you should aim to hold  each contraction for around six seconds  you should train multiple joint angles  as the area of effect is only somewhere  between 10 to 30 degrees and you should  avoid using overcoming isometrics for  more than 10 minutes total during a  workout but now we're going to take this  concept even further with two additional  forms of isometrics  so the first is ballistic isometrics and  these are sometimes also referred to as  explosive isometrics either way you  might be thinking that they sound a  little bit contradictory  i've mentioned these before we're going  to be using them in a slightly different  way now  the idea is that you're going to try and  recruit max effort as quickly as  possible against the immovable object  exploding into that effort and thereby  potentially increasing your starting  strength and the ability to quickly  reach top power for explosive movements  you can of course wrap these out holding  the contraction for only very brief  periods or you can combine the concepts  so that you start the contraction as  explosively as possible and then hold it  for a little while after but there's  another interesting way to incorporate  ballistic isometrics too because if you  generate as much force as you can for  around six seconds measuring it with a  hand dynamic to say you will inevitably  find that it starts to taper off towards  the end as your muscle fibers fatigue  and give out if you started at 60  kilograms you might finish at a much  lower 48 kilograms but now try making  conscious effort to explosively contract  again during that contraction and you'll  find you actually do have a little force  left in the tank that you weren't using  you can bump it back up to say 55  kilograms in other words by adding a  ballistic isometric during a longer  overcoming isometric you can recruit  even more muscle fiber why because the  nervous system never allows you to  recruit 100 of your muscle fiber or even  100 of your fast twitch containing motor  units  there are always some left in reserve to  prevent injury and to conserve energy so  as you start to fatigue the motor units  you're currently using you can send a  new neural drive to recruit even more  large ones in doing so you dig deeper  recruit more strength and force more  neural adaptations so this is a great  hack for building even more strength  from isometrics  at the other end of the spectrum we have  quasi-isometrics which have much more in  common with yielding isometrics  the idea in this case is to move through  a complete range of motion as slowly as  possible potentially taking minutes to  complete a pull-up or press-up this is  much harder than it may at first sound  but it does numerous things firstly it  trains you to much more finely control  the amount of force that you're exerting  during the movement you need to recruit  and or release just the right amount of  motor units to gradually raise or lower  your body gives you much more precise  control  secondly it forces you to maintain full  concentration through the entire range  of motion and to feel that feedback from  your muscles as you do many people say  that this can help to fix technique  issues  finally it forces you to contract your  muscles continuously during the  stretched position which is well known  to elicit hypertrophy and strength gains  in short if overcoming an explosive  isometrics build brute force and power  quasi isometrics can be used to develop  finesse and precision combine both and  you can begin to control your muscles in  a way that even strength athletes rarely  manage  to get started aim for around thirty  percent of your one rep max or use body  weight to avoid injury then aim for a 30  second contraction and the same for  eccentric  obviously if you can't manage that right  away 15 seconds 20 seconds that's fine  this is very difficult and you do want  to build up to it  during the lowering phase you can choose  to use either active or passive  contractions active means that you're  engaging as much force and muscle  control as possible passive means you're  attempting to relax and use only the  necessary strength  the former is better for hypertrophy  effectively being very similar to a  negative rep or a weighted stretch the  latter can improve strength and control  and also help you to improve your  endurance and efficiency it's a  fantastic tool for rock climbers for  instance  when using these avoid extremely complex  movements that put you in a compromised  position this technique is not suitable  for deadlifts for instance a good way to  use them is at the end of a workout as a  kind of finisher you can also just do a  whole session of quasi isometrics if you  want to mix things up  these aren't the only methods for  improving muscle control and fiber  recruitment there's lots more i want to  look into in future including maxik and  the max solving method electromyo  stimulation and of course i've got tons  of other stuff on the way as usual that  digital polymath series a video on  assassin training uh the next part of  the nightwing workout so if that all  sounds good then thanks a ton for  watching and i'll speak to you next time  bye for now and by the way this video is  partly a response to a request from  viking sloth in the comments so a shout  out to him and thanks a ton for the  suggestion
 

